rs  time because we absolutely don't know  the answer to that question what we can  absolutely say and of course you're  right this is a taxpayer-funded system  it is quite rightly led by politicians  who have to account in the house of  commons for how that money is spent and  of course all of our trust leaders are  absolutely focused on delivering value  for money because they know that this is  taxpayers money and that they should be  held accountable for how much they  deliver for that money and that's  precisely why we have made these much  more detailed commitments than were made  um in the initial announcement where as  i said we committed to a level of  elective activity around 130 percent by  uh 2425 while we've done more work to  try and give patients a clearer  indication uh in with a degree of  specificity for example breaking out  cancer about um when they can expect to  be treated because that that's part of  being held accountable i i just the bit  that if i'm honest kate i found  difficult about the article that you  wrote is the sort of implication that  sits behind it that somehow nhs trust  leaders are kind of seeking to evade  accountability they're seeking to hide  information they're seeking to  effectively as i said be dragged into  setting an ambitious uh target nothing  could be further from the truth every  single chief executive that i talk to is  really clear about the moral imperative  here about treating these uh backlogs as  quickly as they possibly can because day  in day out they are talking to patients  and they are seeing patients who are in  pain so they are going absolutely as  fast as they can and they're absolutely  making stretching as far stretching as  far as they can to meet these targets  but they also quite rightly are saying  they have to be deliverable so i don't  quite see this picture of somehow a  reluctant nhs being dragged into making  commitments when actually it's those  trust leaders who want to make as  stretching  commitments as possible because they  know the impact on patients if they  don't make those commitments well i  don't at all pretend to know it's in the  hearts of the nhs officials who are  negotiating this just looking at the  details of what was once thought to be  agreed at least on the political side  was that if you go back to summer 2021  there were  modeling there was modeling coming out  from the department of health showing  that hopefully by 2025 you would have a  waitlist that was closer to 5.5 million  based on the tax rise the substantial  manifesto breaking tax rise that  politicians were considering and somehow  you get just a few months down the road  and that's gone up by millions in the  situation three years time is forecast  to look a lot worse than it does now and  i know that that information wasn't  wanted in the public domain so again  it's very reasonable to say that you  know you think the nhs is doing its best  it's reasonable to say that we don't  want to make promises we're not sure we  can keep but this elective recovery plan  has been based off these models based  off this time frame which has not been  published and again as you point out for  the patients who are in pain who are  suffering many who are  going to really struggle until they get  access to some of the selective care  that they need  shouldn't they have a reasonable idea of  the time frame that the nhs is looking  at behind closed doors shouldn't that be  opened so that the patients themselves  at least know what the nhs in this  recovery plan is based off of their  assumptions at the moment even if those  assumptions change because of course  that can be updated  well again i'm afraid i don't quite  recognize the narrative here in terms of  um and i've spoken to the people who on  the nhs side have been doing the  detailed discussions and and where we  were was that exactly as you say last um  at the point last september-ish whenever  the government made their announcement  around the extra money for the nhs we  did a lot of careful work to agree a  specific top level uh activity target  around 130 percent of pre-covered  elective activity the work that we've  then been doing since is exactly what  you just said we're keen to avoid which  is actually to how do you turn that into  some commitments for patients because we  know that patients aren't interested  about how much activity the nhs is  getting through what they want to know  is when are they going to get their  treatment and that's precisely why  earlier on this week we announced a set  of detailed and they are very stretching  they are challenging ambitions but the  crucial bit is we know that they're  deliverable and so i just don't  recognize and neither do the people  who've been working on this from the nhs  side recognize a narrative that says  that we've been trying to hide things  there have been kind of sort of secret  uh pieces of analysis done that haven't  been put in the public domain what we've  put in the public domain is rightly what  we should have done which is  stretching  deliverable ambitious but clear targets  about when people can expect to be  treated  well the forecasts are now in the public  domain you can read about them in the  spectator magazine this week and i  suppose the public can make up their  minds as to whether or not they thought  that should have been published along  with the recovery plan but let me ask  you are you confident now that the nhs  officials and the government departments  are broadly on the same page about the  metrics that need to be achieved over  the next few years when it comes to the  backlog and many of the other aspects  laid out in the recovery plan because i  know uh from your thread on twitter that  it's about achieving those metrics and  those targets that matters a lot to you  yes i mean so um there was some  interesting kind of uh speculation  earlier on in the week as to why there  was a delay in the